# Run package scripts with bash on every platform, including Windows.
#
# pnpm defaults to `cmd.exe` on Windows and `/bin/sh` elsewhere. The scripts in
# this workspace are written in POSIX shell and use, among other things:
#
#   data-browser "build"       if [ "$SKIP_WASM_BUILD" = "1" ]; then ... fi
#   data-browser "build:wasm"  export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=... && env -u ... && cp
#   data-browser "build:tauri" TAURI=1 vite build
#
# None of that is cmd.exe syntax, so on Windows the first one fails with
# `"$SKIP_WASM_BUILD" was unexpected at this time` and the rest would follow.
# That is what blocked the Windows Tauri build.
#
# Pinning bash rather than rewriting four release-critical scripts in Node: it
# fixes the whole class in one line, needs no new dependency, and leaves the
# macOS/Linux path — which the crates.io release depends on — untouched.
#
# It also hardens the Unix side. `/bin/sh` is dash on Debian and Ubuntu, and a
# bashism that works on a developer's macOS (where sh is bash) fails there. That
# is not hypothetical: `cargo bin wasm-pack ... $--cfg\x1f...` broke the
# v0.41.0-beta.2 release exactly that way. This makes local and CI agree.
#
# Requires bash on PATH. Present on every GitHub runner including
# windows-latest (Git for Windows ships it), and on any Windows machine with
# Git installed — which anyone building this repo needs anyway.
script-shell=bash
